1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,000
You're eighteen, you live in the Bronx, and the Red Nation wants you.

2
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:09,890
This is what the next year of your life looks like if you say yes.

3
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:16,990
Not the version from music videos. The version from federal court records, police data, and decades of research on gangs.

4
00:00:17,450 --> 00:00:20,970
And it starts with a number that surprises most people.

5
00:00:21,417 --> 00:00:28,617
Researchers who followed young people in the United States and in Europe found that the most common length of a gang membership is about one year.

6
00:00:29,083 --> 00:00:30,683
One year.

7
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:36,750
So the question isn't whether this lasts. It's how your year ends, and what it costs you.

8
00:00:37,217 --> 00:00:39,697
One more thing before we start.

9
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:49,910
This isn't a guide, and it isn't a tribute. Every claim about a crime in this video comes from a court record or a study, and the sources are in the description.

10
00:00:50,350 --> 00:00:53,230
The Red Nation didn't start on a street corner.

11
00:00:53,683 --> 00:00:55,363
They started in a jail.

12
00:00:55,817 --> 00:01:01,257
In 1993, on Rikers Island, an inmate founded what became the United Red Nation.

13
00:01:01,717 --> 00:01:07,877
The way his followers told it, it was about protection, for Black inmates who were outnumbered by other gangs.

14
00:01:08,317 --> 00:01:12,717
The way federal prosecutors put it, he founded it "to amass power."

15
00:01:13,183 --> 00:01:16,463
Keep both versions in mind. You're about to hear the first one.

16
00:01:18,267 --> 00:01:23,707
It's June. School's out. Your mother works nights, and your little brother follows you everywhere.

17
00:01:24,150 --> 00:01:28,790
There's an older guy on your block. Everyone knows him. He's always been decent to you.

18
00:01:29,250 --> 00:01:34,530
Lately, he's been asking how you're doing. Whether anyone's been bothering you. Whether you need money.

19
00:01:34,983 --> 00:01:38,743
Nobody says the word "recruiting." Nobody has to.

20
00:01:39,183 --> 00:01:45,343
What he's offering is exactly what researchers and police analysts list as the reasons people join.

21
00:01:45,783 --> 00:01:50,503
Protection. Status. Money. And a sense of belonging.

22
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:58,230
In a long study of teenagers in Rochester, New York, focused on high-risk neighborhoods, almost a third joined a gang at some point.

23
00:01:58,683 --> 00:02:02,603
Across the United States, the age when joining peaks is fourteen.

24
00:02:03,050 --> 00:02:05,130
At eighteen, you're actually late.

25
00:02:05,583 --> 00:02:08,543
And here's the part nobody on your block mentions.

26
00:02:08,983 --> 00:02:15,623
A study that followed gang members over time found that the ones who joined for protection stayed in longer, and were victimized more.

27
00:02:16,083 --> 00:02:20,243
The ones who joined for money were more likely to be out within a year.

28
00:02:20,683 --> 00:02:23,963
Protection is the promise. It's also the trap.

29
00:02:24,417 --> 00:02:26,577
One night in July, you say yes.

30
00:02:28,367 --> 00:02:31,647
Getting in usually means a test of loyalty.

31
00:02:32,083 --> 00:02:36,963
Police intelligence reports describe new members being beaten by several members at once.

32
00:02:37,417 --> 00:02:40,057
Court records describe something worse.

33
00:02:40,517 --> 00:02:49,317
In 1999, according to federal prosecutors, the founder of the Red Nation ordered a teenager to shoot a rival, and told him it would be his initiation.

34
00:02:49,783 --> 00:02:54,583
The man who was shot survived. The founder was later convicted for it, along with much more.

35
00:02:55,050 --> 00:02:59,930
That's the trade, from day one. The gang asks for something you can't take back.

36
00:03:00,383 --> 00:03:04,623
And something else happens the moment you're in. It happens on paper.

37
00:03:05,083 --> 00:03:08,043
The New York Police Department keeps a gang database.

38
00:03:08,483 --> 00:03:14,163
At the end of 2022, it held sixteen thousand, one hundred and forty-one names.

39
00:03:14,617 --> 00:03:17,737
Ninety-nine percent of them were Black or Hispanic.

40
00:03:18,183 --> 00:03:25,463
And the most common reason for being added was the simplest one: the person said it. To an officer, or on social media.

41
00:03:25,917 --> 00:03:31,437
The city's own investigators found that the police didn't give officers clear enough rules for who gets added.

42
00:03:31,883 --> 00:03:35,483
So the first time you post about it, you may already be on a list.

43
00:03:35,950 --> 00:03:41,390
If you want more stories told from the records instead of the myths, that's what the subscribe button is for.

44
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:46,800
September. Membership comes with obligations.

45
00:03:47,250 --> 00:03:54,690
In federal indictments, prosecutors describe members paying dues, and being expected to commit crimes to keep their place and to move up.

46
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:57,950
And the people giving orders aren't always on the street.

47
00:03:58,417 --> 00:04:06,977
In one Manhattan case, prosecutors described a gang with two leaderships: a street lineup, and a prison lineup made up of members behind bars.

48
00:04:07,417 --> 00:04:10,537
The thing you joined still answers to a jail.

49
00:04:10,983 --> 00:04:13,383
You start doing things you wouldn't have done in May.

50
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:16,250
The research says that isn't an accident.

51
00:04:16,717 --> 00:04:22,797
In the Rochester study, gang members were about a third of the teenagers, and committed about two thirds of the offenses.

52
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:30,210
One of the researchers said that gun use, in his words, "goes up dramatically" when adolescents join gangs.

53
00:04:30,650 --> 00:04:31,850
Then there's your phone.

54
00:04:32,317 --> 00:04:38,317
Researchers have a name for gang conflict that plays out online: "internet banging."

55
00:04:38,783 --> 00:04:42,783
Bragging, insults and threats, posted where everyone can see them.

56
00:04:43,250 --> 00:04:47,890
A post that takes ten seconds to write can follow you into neighborhoods you've never set foot in.

57
00:04:48,350 --> 00:04:51,310
And remember the database. Posts count.

58
00:04:51,750 --> 00:04:55,670
By the end of September, there are blocks you don't walk down anymore.

59
00:04:57,467 --> 00:04:58,427
November.

60
00:04:58,883 --> 00:05:03,283
Here's something most people don't know about getting shot in an American city.

61
00:05:03,750 --> 00:05:04,950
It isn't random.

62
00:05:05,417 --> 00:05:08,937
In Chicago, researchers mapped six years of shootings.

63
00:05:09,383 --> 00:05:17,223
Seventy percent of the people who survived a gunshot belonged to networks of people arrested together that made up less than six percent of the city.

64
00:05:17,683 --> 00:05:25,203
In Boston, another study found that the closer you are to a gunshot victim in your social network, the higher your own odds of being shot.

65
00:05:25,650 --> 00:05:29,650
Your risk doesn't depend only on what you do. It depends on who you know.

66
00:05:30,117 --> 00:05:32,517
And now you know a lot of the wrong people.

67
00:05:32,983 --> 00:05:42,503
Researchers who studied gang members in Texas prisons found the same pattern behind bars: members were far more likely to be victims of violence than non-members.

68
00:05:42,950 --> 00:05:48,790
In November, someone you came up with is shot. He lives. You find out from a post.

69
00:05:49,250 --> 00:05:51,570
Your little brother saw it before you did.

70
00:05:52,017 --> 00:05:54,497
And for the first time, you think about getting out.

71
00:05:54,950 --> 00:06:00,950
You've heard the rule a hundred times. Blood in, blood out. Nobody leaves alive.

72
00:06:01,417 --> 00:06:03,417
Is that true? We'll get there.

73
00:06:05,233 --> 00:06:06,913
March. Six in the morning.

74
00:06:07,350 --> 00:06:10,310
Someone is pounding on your mother's door.

75
00:06:10,750 --> 00:06:15,310
It isn't a rival. It's federal agents and the NYPD.

76
00:06:15,750 --> 00:06:21,350
In 2025 alone, the NYPD carried out seventy gang takedowns like this one.

77
00:06:21,817 --> 00:06:26,457
Your mother is standing in the hallway in her work clothes. Your brother is behind her.

78
00:06:26,917 --> 00:06:29,557
And you learn a new word. RICO.

79
00:06:30,017 --> 00:06:33,057
It's a federal law originally aimed at organized crime.

80
00:06:33,517 --> 00:06:41,357
Under it, a group of people can be charged as a criminal enterprise, with no office and no paperwork, just the fact that they act together.

81
00:06:41,817 --> 00:06:46,297
Prosecutors have to show a pattern: at least two crimes, tied to that enterprise.

82
00:06:46,750 --> 00:06:52,030
So the case isn't only about what you did. It's about the whole group, and your part in it.

83
00:06:52,483 --> 00:06:54,083
Then there are the guns.

84
00:06:54,550 --> 00:07:04,150
Under federal law, using or carrying a gun during a violent crime or a drug deal adds at least five years. Seven if it's brandished. Ten if it's fired.

85
00:07:04,583 --> 00:07:09,863
And those years don't run at the same time as the rest of the sentence. They come after it.

86
00:07:10,317 --> 00:07:17,037
This is exactly what happened to a set called the Nine-Three, in Manhattan, starting in 2018.

87
00:07:17,483 --> 00:07:24,523
Federal prosecutors charged six members and associates in November. By the end of January, there were ten.

88
00:07:24,983 --> 00:07:28,503
One defendant who pleaded guilty was sentenced to fifteen years.

89
00:07:28,950 --> 00:07:32,550
A high-ranking member who went to trial got twenty-four.

90
00:07:33,017 --> 00:07:37,977
And the best-known defendant in the case was a rapper with millions of fans.

91
00:07:38,417 --> 00:07:40,977
What he did next is the choice in front of you now.

92
00:07:42,767 --> 00:07:45,407
In April, your lawyer brings you an offer.

93
00:07:45,850 --> 00:07:50,170
Testify against the others, and the government will ask the judge for less time.

94
00:07:50,617 --> 00:07:56,617
In the Nine-Three case, prosecutors said the charges against that rapper carried at least forty-seven years.

95
00:07:57,083 --> 00:08:00,203
He cooperated, and he testified at trial.

96
00:08:00,650 --> 00:08:02,650
He was sentenced to two years.

97
00:08:03,117 --> 00:08:11,197
Prosecutors said that once his cooperation became public, several of his co-defendants contacted the government to start plea talks of their own.

98
00:08:11,650 --> 00:08:15,090
And his family had to be moved before anyone found out.

99
00:08:15,550 --> 00:08:21,790
That's the offer. Decades of your life back, in exchange for being the one who talked, for the rest of your life.

100
00:08:22,250 --> 00:08:26,330
Cooperate, or go to trial? Tell me in the comments what you would do.

101
00:08:28,133 --> 00:08:32,853
While you think about it, let's settle that rule. Blood in, blood out.

102
00:08:33,317 --> 00:08:39,957
David Pyrooz, a criminologist at the University of Colorado, studied hundreds of men in Texas prisons.

103
00:08:40,417 --> 00:08:44,977
His conclusion: "This idea of blood in, blood out is a myth."

104
00:08:45,417 --> 00:08:49,737
Most of the men who had joined a gang inside had already left it before they were released.

105
00:08:50,183 --> 00:08:52,583
On the outside, the numbers look the same.

106
00:08:53,050 --> 00:09:01,770
Every year in the United States, researchers estimate, about four hundred thousand young people join a gang, and almost as many leave.

107
00:09:02,217 --> 00:09:05,737
So yes. People walk away. Most of them do.

108
00:09:06,183 --> 00:09:09,303
But leaving the gang isn't the same as leaving the danger.

109
00:09:09,750 --> 00:09:17,670
Another study found that people who stay tied to their old gang friends after leaving keep getting victimized, and that risk fades only with time.

110
00:09:18,117 --> 00:09:21,877
The way out is real. It just takes years to close behind you.

111
00:09:23,667 --> 00:09:25,267
So how does your year end?

112
00:09:25,717 --> 00:09:30,917
Exactly the way the research said it would. About a year after you said yes, you're out.

113
00:09:31,383 --> 00:09:34,823
Not because you walked away. Because the government took you out.

114
00:09:35,283 --> 00:09:42,403
You're nineteen, in a federal jail in Brooklyn, with an offer on the table, and a possible sentence longer than your life so far.

115
00:09:42,850 --> 00:09:49,010
Fifteen years. Twenty-four. Or two, and your mother and your brother moving somewhere nobody knows.

116
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:52,480
And the city you left behind?

117
00:09:52,917 --> 00:10:00,357
In 2025, New York had six hundred and eighty-eight shooting incidents, the fewest in its recorded history.

118
00:10:00,817 --> 00:10:05,057
In 2022, it had been one thousand, two hundred and ninety-four.

119
00:10:05,517 --> 00:10:11,837
Across the country, researchers estimate that only about two percent of young people are in a gang at any given time.

120
00:10:12,283 --> 00:10:14,683
Most people your age never join anything.

121
00:10:15,150 --> 00:10:18,510
And the founder, the man who said it was all about protection?

122
00:10:18,950 --> 00:10:24,870
A federal jury convicted him on ten counts, including racketeering and conspiracy to commit murder.

123
00:10:25,317 --> 00:10:30,437
In 2003, he was sentenced to fifty years in federal prison.

124
00:10:30,883 --> 00:10:34,083
It started in a jail, with a promise of protection.

125
00:10:34,550 --> 00:10:36,390
For him, it ended in one.

126
00:10:36,850 --> 00:10:39,410
For you, it has been exactly one year.
